Sense of Direction - Concept & Step-by-Step Approach
Given a string representing a series of movement instructions using cardinal directions (North, South, East, West), determine the final sense of direction relative to the starting point. Each character in the string represents a unit movement:

'N' for one unit North
'S' for one unit South
'E' for one unit East
'W' for one unit West
Constraints:

The string length can be any non-negative integer (including zero).
Movements are independent and additive.
The output should be the primary cardinal direction (or a diagonal if applicable) that corresponds to the net displacement.
Section 2 — Intuition
The core insight is that every movement has an opposing counterpart. North (N) and South (S) movements cancel each other out to some degree, as do East (E) and West (W) movements. By calculating the net vertical displacement (difference between counts of 'N' and 'S') and the net horizontal displacement (difference between counts of 'E' and 'W'), we can determine the overall direction. If one axis has a zero displacement, the final direction is along the other axis; if both axes have non-zero displacement, the direction is diagonal (e.g., "North-East").

Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force
Explanation
Iterate over the entire string and update vertical and horizontal counters one by one. Then derive the final direction based on the net counters.

Code
